\title{Chromatic Positivity Thresholds for Emotion-Labeled Social Networks}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We study a constrained-labeling model in which vertices of a finite simple graph represent people, edges represent friendships, and adjacent vertices must receive distinct labels from an emotional palette. The chromatic counting function $P_G(k)$ counts proper assignments from a palette of size $k$. We define the emotional chromatic number $\tau_E(G)$ as the least $k\geq3$ for which a proper assignment exists. We prove that for every $k\geq3$, $\tau_E(G)\leq k$ if and only if $P_G(k)>0$. Consequently, $\tau_E(G)=k$ precisely when $k\geq3$, $P_G(k)>0$, and $P_G(j)=0$ for every $3\leq j<k$. In particular, $P_G(6)>0$ if and only if $3\leq\tau_E(G)\leq6$. For the friendship graph $F_n$, formed from $n$ triangles sharing a hub, we derive $P_{F_n}(k)=k(k-1)^n(k-2)^n$. Hence $\tau_E(F_n)=3$, $P_{F_n}(3)=3\cdot2^n$, and $P_{F_n}(6)=6\cdot20^n$. We give algorithms, applications, limitations, and directions for further work.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Graph coloring models local incompatibility. One assigns labels to vertices so that adjacent vertices receive different labels. The smallest number of labels required is the chromatic number, while the chromatic polynomial records how many assignments are available from a palette of a specified size.

We specialize this framework to social networks. Vertices represent people, edges represent friendship, and labels are called emotions. A proper assignment requires friends to receive different labels. With six labels, one may use happiness, sadness, anger, fear, disgust, and surprise. This terminology is metaphorical: real emotions are neither exclusive colors nor ordinarily constrained to differ across friendships. The model is a rigorous illustration of constrained labeling, not a psychological law.

Our definition imposes a palette floor of three. This makes the resulting parameter a truncated chromatic threshold. The central result identifies it with the first positive integer evaluation of the chromatic counting function on the admissible range. Thus an order-theoretic minimum and an enumerative invariant encode the same boundary between impossibility and feasibility.

We also study friendship graphs, or windmill graphs. Their triangles interact only through one central vertex, producing exact multiplicative counts. This family shows that the minimum palette and the number of assignments are distinct statistics: the threshold remains three, while the number of six-label assignments grows as $6\cdot20^n$.

\section{Definitions and elementary principles}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{finite simple graph} is a pair $G=(V,E)$, where $V$ is finite and $E$ consists of unordered pairs of distinct vertices. We write $u\sim v$ when $\{u,v\}\in E$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}
For a nonnegative integer $k$, a \emph{proper $k$-assignment} is a function
\[
c:V\longrightarrow\{1,\ldots,k\}
\]
such that $c(u)\neq c(v)$ whenever $u\sim v$. A graph is $k$-colorable when such an assignment exists.
\end{definition}

If a graph is $r$-colorable and $r\leq k$, it is $k$-colorable: retain the assignment and leave the additional labels unused. We call this palette monotonicity.

\begin{definition}
The \emph{chromatic counting function} is
\[
P_G(k)=\#\{c:V\to\{1,\ldots,k\}:c\text{ is proper}\}.
\]
For finite graphs this agrees at nonnegative integers with the chromatic polynomial.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Positivity and colorability]
For every finite simple graph $G$ and every nonnegative integer $k$,
\[
P_G(k)>0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad G\text{ is }k\text{-colorable}.
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
The count $P_G(k)$ is the cardinality of the finite set of proper assignments. It is positive exactly when this set is nonempty, which is precisely $k$-colorability.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Emotional chromatic number]
For a finite simple graph $G$, define
\[
\tau_E(G)=\min\{k\in\mathbb N:k\geq3\text{ and }G\text{ is }k\text{-colorable}\}.
\]
\end{definition}

The set is nonempty because distinct labels can be assigned to all vertices and the palette can be enlarged to at least three. If $\chi(G)$ is the ordinary chromatic number, then
\[
\tau_E(G)=\max\{3,\chi(G)\}.
\]
Thus the emotional parameter is exactly the ordinary threshold truncated below by three.

\begin{lemma}[Floor and attainment]
Every finite simple graph $G$ satisfies $\tau_E(G)\geq3$, and $G$ has a proper $\tau_E(G)$-assignment.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both assertions follow from membership of the minimum in the nonempty defining set.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Bipartite graphs]
It is false that every bipartite graph has $P_G(2)=0$. A single edge has exactly two proper two-assignments, obtained by swapping its two labels. Bipartiteness implies two-colorability and hence positivity at two. The floor at three is a modeling convention, not a consequence of bipartiteness.
\end{remark}

The floor also changes standard examples. Every cycle $C_n$ with $n\geq3$ has $\tau_E(C_n)=3$, although its ordinary chromatic number is two for even $n$ and three for odd $n$. Similarly, $\tau_E(K_n)=\max\{3,n\}$, so the simpler expression $\tau_E(K_n)=n$ applies when $n\geq3$.

\section{The positivity threshold}

\begin{theorem}[Emotional Threshold Theorem]
Let $G$ be a finite simple graph and $k\geq3$. Then
\[
\tau_E(G)\leq k\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad G\text{ is }k\text{-colorable}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $\tau_E(G)\leq k$, attainment supplies a proper assignment at $\tau_E(G)$ and palette monotonicity enlarges it to a $k$-palette. Conversely, if $G$ is $k$-colorable and $k\geq3$, then $k$ belongs to the set minimized in the definition, so its minimum is no larger than $k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Positivity-threshold equivalence]
For every finite simple graph $G$ and $k\geq3$,
\[
\tau_E(G)\leq k\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad P_G(k)>0.
\]
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Combine the Emotional Threshold Theorem with positivity and colorability.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Minimal-Positive-Value Theorem]
For every finite simple graph $G$ and every nonnegative integer $k$, the equality $\tau_E(G)=k$ holds if and only if
\[
k\geq3,\qquad P_G(k)>0,
\]
and
\[
P_G(j)=0\quad\text{for every integer }j\text{ with }3\leq j<k.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Assume $\tau_E(G)=k$. The floor gives $k\geq3$, and attainment gives $P_G(k)>0$. If an admissible $j<k$ had positive count, the positivity-threshold equivalence would imply $\tau_E(G)\leq j<k$, a contradiction. Since counts are nonnegative integers, each earlier count is zero.

Conversely, positivity at $k$ gives $\tau_E(G)\leq k$. If the inequality were strict, set $j=\tau_E(G)$. The floor gives $j\geq3$, strictness gives $j<k$, and attainment gives $P_G(j)>0$, contradicting the assumed vanishing. Hence equality holds.
\end{proof}

This theorem says that $\tau_E(G)$ is the first positive member of
\[
P_G(3),P_G(4),P_G(5),\ldots.
\]
The preceding admissible values form a contiguous interval of zeros. This concerns integer evaluations, not all algebraic roots over the complex numbers.

\begin{theorem}[Six-Emotion Characterization]
For every finite simple graph $G$,
\[
P_G(6)>0\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad3\leq\tau_E(G)\leq6.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The lower inequality always holds by definition. The upper inequality is equivalent to $P_G(6)>0$ by the positivity-threshold equivalence at $k=6$.
\end{proof}

This is an exact test, not a universal assertion that six labels suffice. For instance, $K_7$ requires seven distinct labels and therefore has $P_{K_7}(6)=0$.

\section{Friendship graphs}

\begin{definition}
For $n\geq0$, the \emph{friendship graph} $F_n$ consists of a hub $h$ and pairs of outer vertices $a_i,b_i$ for $1\leq i\leq n$, with edges $\{h,a_i\}$, $\{h,b_i\}$, and $\{a_i,b_i\}$. Thus $F_n$ is formed from $n$ triangles sharing exactly one common vertex. For $n=0$, only the hub remains.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Friendship chromatic formula]
For every $n,k\geq0$,
\[
P_{F_n}(k)=k(k-1)^n(k-2)^n
=k\bigl((k-1)(k-2)\bigr)^n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose the hub label in $k$ ways. In each triangle, $a_i$ has $k-1$ choices because it differs from the hub. Then $b_i$ has $k-2$ choices because it differs from both the hub and $a_i$. Different outer pairs have no edges between them, so these choices are independent. Multiplication gives the formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Friendship-Network Threshold Profile]
For every $n\geq0$,
\[
\tau_E(F_n)=3,
\qquad P_{F_n}(3)=3\cdot2^n,
\qquad P_{F_n}(6)=6\cdot20^n.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
At $k=3$, the friendship formula gives
\[
P_{F_n}(3)=3(2)^n(1)^n=3\cdot2^n>0.
\]
The threshold equivalence gives $\tau_E(F_n)\leq3$, and the floor gives the reverse inequality. At $k=6$ the formula gives
\[
P_{F_n}(6)=6(5)^n(4)^n=6\cdot20^n.
\]
\end{proof}

The ratio between the six-label and minimum-palette counts is
\[
\frac{P_{F_n}(6)}{P_{F_n}(3)}=2\cdot10^n.
\]
Each new triangle multiplies the three-label count by $2$ but the six-label count by $20$. Thus a fixed threshold can coexist with rapidly expanding assignment abundance.

For $n=1$, the graph is a triangle and the respective counts are $6$ and $120$. For $n=2$, they are $12$ and $2400$. For $n=5$, they are $96$ and $19{,}200{,}000$.

\section{Algorithms}

The Minimal-Positive-Value Theorem gives a threshold-search algorithm. Beginning with $k=3$, test $k$-colorability and return the first successful palette. A depth-first backtracking implementation labels vertices one at a time, abandoning a partial assignment whenever it gives equal labels to adjacent vertices. If $N=|V|$, a naive $k$-test examines at most $k^N$ complete assignments. Through an upper bound $U$, the worst-case running time is
\[
O\left(|E|\sum_{k=3}^{U}k^N\right),
\]
with substantial practical savings from pruning and good vertex orderings. The bound $U=\max\{3,N\}$ always suffices.

To evaluate $P_G(k)$ exactly, the same traversal counts rather than stops at proper assignments. Its naive time is $O(|E|k^N)$ and recursive space is $O(N)$. This is appropriate for small examples.

For friendship graphs, direct evaluation of $k((k-1)(k-2))^n$ avoids enumeration. Exponentiation by squaring uses $O(\log n)$ arithmetic multiplications, with additional bit cost reflecting the exponentially large output.

\section{Applications and limitations}

The mathematics applies to any graph-based incompatibility problem. Adjacent transmitters may require distinct frequencies; conflicting examinations may require distinct time slots; simultaneously active variables may require distinct registers. In each case, a threshold records the minimum resource level, while the chromatic count records the abundance of feasible configurations.

The emotional interpretation offers an accessible narrative but not a literal psychological mechanism. It compresses friendship to a binary edge, emotion to one exclusive category, and consistency to adjacent inequality. Real affect is multidimensional and dynamic. Numerical conclusions therefore describe the chosen labeling model.

A study of one hundred observed networks could test whether most satisfy $3\leq\tau_E(G)\leq6$. By the Six-Emotion Characterization, this is exactly the same as testing whether $P_G(6)>0$ on most samples. The claim remains empirical: it depends on sampling, network size, and the definition of an edge. A seven-clique is an immediate obstruction to six-colorability, though absence of such a clique is not sufficient because global chromatic complexity need not arise from a large clique.

\section{Discussion and future work}

The framework distinguishes existence, threshold, and abundance. Existence asks whether $P_G(k)>0$. Threshold asks where positivity first appears on the admissible range. Abundance asks how large $P_G(k)$ is thereafter. The first two coincide at the decision boundary, but the friendship formulas show that abundance contains much more information.

Several directions follow. Clique-minor structure may be studied through intervals of forced zero evaluations, reframing Hadwiger-type lower bounds as vanishing certificates. For fixed $k\geq3$, deciding $\tau_E(G)\leq k$ and deciding $P_G(k)>0$ are exactly the same instance-level problem, inviting comparisons among decision, counting, parameterized, and approximation complexity.

High-girth graphs show that locally tree-like geometry need not impose a low global threshold. In the present language, one seeks networks whose admissible chromatic evaluations vanish for a long interval despite having no short cycles. Finally, among connected graphs assembled from triangles, the windmill ratio $2\cdot10^n$ supplies a baseline for measuring entropy loss caused by more complicated overlap. An extremal theory could identify which gluing patterns maximize or minimize the ratio between six-label and minimum-palette counts.

Empirical work could measure threshold distributions in observed networks and compare them with density, community structure, clique number, and assignment counts. Such studies should keep the mathematical equivalence separate from claims about human behavior.

\section{Conclusion}
For every finite graph, the emotional chromatic number is the ordinary chromatic threshold truncated below at three. Above that floor,
\[
\tau_E(G)\leq k\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad P_G(k)>0.
\]
The exact value is characterized by positivity at the selected palette and vanishing at every smaller admissible palette. Six labels suffice exactly when $P_G(6)>0$.

For friendship graphs,
\[
P_{F_n}(k)=k(k-1)^n(k-2)^n,
\]
so
\[
\tau_E(F_n)=3,\qquad P_{F_n}(3)=3\cdot2^n,
\qquad P_{F_n}(6)=6\cdot20^n.
\]
The first positive admissible evaluation marks the onset of possibility; later evaluations measure the widening landscape of choice.

