# A Guided Tour of Emotional Chromatic Thresholds

A network can be viewed as a collection of vertices joined by incompatibility edges. A palette assignment is proper when neighboring vertices receive distinct labels. The [chromatic polynomial](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chromatic_polynomial) counts these assignments, while the emotional chromatic number is the first workable palette constrained to have at least three labels.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

## See the windmill geometry

Friendship graphs consist of triangles sharing a single hub. Explore their structure before examining the exact formula.

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal the counting argument</summary>
Choose the hub label in $k$ ways. In each of $n$ triangles, the first outer vertex has $k-1$ choices and its partner has $k-2$ choices. Independent outer pairs multiply, giving $P_{F_n}(k)=k(k-1)^n(k-2)^n$.
</details>

{{algorithm:2}}

## Where positivity begins

The emotional threshold is the first positive value among $P_G(3),P_G(4),\ldots$. Experiment with that boundary.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the minimal-positive theorem</summary>
For $k\ge3$, the equality $	au_E(G)=k$ holds exactly when $P_G(k)>0$ and all values $P_G(j)$ with $3\le j<k$ vanish. Positivity gives a coloring at $k$; the preceding zeros rule out every smaller admissible palette.
</details>

{{algorithm:0}}

## Three labels versus six

The windmill always reaches its admissible threshold at three, with $3\cdot2^n$ assignments. Six labels produce $6\cdot20^n$ assignments and the ratio is $2\cdot10^n$.

{{visualization:0}}

{{demo:0}}

{{demo:1}}

## The six-label decision boundary

Six labels suffice exactly when $P_G(6)>0$, equivalently when $3\le	au_E(G)\le6$. A friendship graph passes for every $n$, whereas the clique $K_7$ fails.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{demo:2}}

<details><summary>Background: ordinary versus emotional chromatic number</summary>
The ordinary [chromatic number](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Graph_coloring) is $\chi(G)$. The emotional convention imposes a floor, so $	au_E(G)=\max\{3,\chi(G)\}$. Thus even cycles have ordinary chromatic number two but emotional chromatic number three. Contrary to a common misstatement, bipartite graphs do not have zero count at two; a single edge has two proper two-assignments.
</details>

## Compute an exact evaluation

